While implementing a CI/CD pipeline for my personal website using GitLab CI/CD and GitLab Pages, I encountered several practical challenges during the setup and deployment process.

The objective was to configure a 𝘴𝘦𝘭𝘧-𝘩𝘰𝘴𝘵𝘦𝘥 𝘎𝘪𝘵𝘓𝘢𝘣 𝘙𝘶𝘯𝘯𝘦𝘳 𝘵𝘰 𝘦𝘹𝘦𝘤𝘶𝘵𝘦 𝘱𝘪𝘱𝘦𝘭𝘪𝘯𝘦 jobs using my personal machine's CPU and memory resources instead of relying on GitLab-hosted runners.

           ~~~~~ 𝙲̲𝚑̲𝚊̲𝚕̲𝚕̲𝚎̲𝚗̲𝚐̲𝚎̲𝚜̲ ̲𝙵̲𝚊̲𝚌̲𝚎̲𝚍̲ ̲&̲ ̲𝚃̲𝚛̲𝚘̲𝚞̲𝚋̲𝚕̲𝚎̲𝚜̲𝚑̲𝚘̲𝚘̲𝚝̲𝚒̲𝚗̲𝚐̲ ~~~~~

1️⃣ 𝗚𝗶𝘁𝗟𝗮𝗯 𝗔𝗰𝗰𝗼𝘂𝗻𝘁 𝗜𝗱𝗲𝗻𝘁𝗶𝘁𝘆 𝗩𝗲𝗿𝗶𝗳𝗶𝗰𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:-

- Pipeline execution was initially blocked due to GitLab's account identity verification requirement.
- GitLab prompted for additional verification, including the option to verify the account using a credit card.
- This led me to explore an alternative approach using a self-hosted runner.

2️⃣ 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗳𝗶𝗴𝘂𝗿𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗮 𝗦𝗲𝗹𝗳-𝗛𝗼𝘀𝘁𝗲𝗱 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗿:-

- Instead of relying on GitLab Instance Runners, configured a self-hosted project runner on my personal machine.
- Disabled GitLab Instance Runners for the project to use local machine resources for CI/CD execution.
- This provided a way to execute pipeline jobs using my own system's CPU and memory.

3️⃣ 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗿 𝗥𝗲𝗴𝗶𝘀𝘁𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻 & 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗰𝘁𝗶𝘃𝗶𝘁𝘆:-

- Registered the self-hosted runner and verified its connectivity using GitLab Runner CLI.
- Identified duplicate runner registrations with the same name.
- Found that one registration was invalid while the other was successfully verified.
- Investigated runner registration and configuration using GitLab Runner commands.

4️⃣ 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗿 𝗢𝗳𝗳𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲 & 𝗣𝗶𝗽𝗲𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲 𝗣𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗜𝘀𝘀𝘂𝗲:-

- Pipeline was created but remained in Pending state because the expected runner was not picking up the jobs.
- Investigated runner availability, project assignment, and untagged-job settings.
- Identified an old runner registration assigned to multiple projects (AI and Test).
- Reviewed runner status and project-level configuration to identify the scheduling issue.

5️⃣ 𝗣𝗼𝘄𝗲𝗿𝗦𝗵𝗲𝗹𝗹 𝗘𝘅𝗲𝗰𝘂𝘁𝗼𝗿 𝗖𝗼𝗻𝗳𝗶𝗴𝘂𝗿𝗮𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:-

- check the Installed PowerShell version and verified it should be 7.6.9  or +  
- Configured the Shell executor with 𝘗𝘰𝘸𝘦𝘳𝘚𝘩𝘦𝘭𝘭 7 (`𝘱𝘸𝘴𝘩`).
- Encountered PowerShell executable availability and compatibility issues.
- Installed PowerShell 7.6.9 and verified its installation and executable path.
- Reviewed `𝘤𝘰𝘯𝘧𝘪𝘨.𝘵𝘰𝘮𝘭` to validate executor and shell configuration.

6️⃣ 𝗣𝗶𝗽𝗲𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗲 𝗦𝘁𝗶𝗹𝗹 𝗣𝗲𝗻𝗱𝗶𝗻𝗴? 𝗖𝗵𝗲𝗰𝗸 𝗥𝘂𝗻𝗻𝗲𝗿 𝗧𝗮𝗴𝘀

- Identified runner tag mismatch as another important configuration to verify.
- If tags are assigned during runner creation, the same tags must be specified in the CI/CD deployment job.
- Updated the `.𝘨𝘪𝘵𝘭𝘢𝘣-𝘤𝘪.𝘺𝘮𝘭` configuration with matching runner tags.
- Committed the changes and triggered the pipeline again.

